<p>Name mismatches matter beyond fraud, because they also stop legitimate payments. Under a letter of credit, the bank checks that the invoice is issued by the beneficiary named in the credit, so a small difference in the name can be called a discrepancy. Scenario: an LC names "Sunrise Agro Exports Pvt Ltd" as beneficiary, but the invoice and IEC are in the name of "Sunrise Agro Exports", a proprietorship run by the same family. The goods have shipped, the bank refuses the documents, and the cargo sits at the destination port while the LC is amended. A one-line check of the IEC name against the draft LC before it is issued would have prevented it. The same check works in reverse: the name on the bank account you are asked to pay by TT should be the name on the IEC and GST record, not a related company.</p>
<p>None of the five checks needs a paid service, and a supplier who answers them promptly has usually been asked before.</p>
